78
00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.180
Source 4 That is an Anthropic evaluation
result.

79
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.740
It can be a useful prompt for research
into

80
00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:09.820
goal conflict, coercion, and model
behavior under artificial pressure.

81
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.700
It is not evidence that Claude blackmailed
a real

82
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.100
employee or that a deployed assistant
independently discovered a

83
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.420
secret and threatened someone.

84
00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.940
A test can be valuable without being a
news

85
00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.500
report. The reader needs to know which one
they

86
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.960
are reading. A reported human
decision-making failure CNN reported

87
00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.960
that an AI-assisted intelligence report
falsely characterized cargo on

88
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.180
a Chinese vessel. According to four people
familiar with

89
00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.140
the episode, armed personnel were
preparing to board and

90
00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.720
aircraft were airborne before further
review stopped the operation.

91
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.220
Source 6 The report does not identify the
AI

92
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:52.220
system. No accessible official record,
public after-action investigation, or

93
00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.540
named primary source independently
confirms the account.

94
00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.380
No clash occurred. That leaves a
potentially important story,

95
00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.240
but not the story that “AI nearly started
a

96
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:07.000
war with China.” The available evidence
supports, at most,

97
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.640
an attributed report of a near-miss
involving human reliance

98
00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.960
on erroneous AI-assisted intelligence.

99
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.300
That distinction places responsibility
where it belongs.

100
00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.820
An AI-generated report can be wrong.

101
00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:21.800
An institution can circulate it.

102
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.240
A human chain of command can act on it.

103
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.420
The system may be part of the failure, but

104
00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.780
the presence of an AI output does not
erase

105
00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:30.820
the people and procedures that accepted
it.

106
00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.840
Capability is not authority A model
completing a task

107
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.260
is not the same as a model having
permission

108
00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.140
to perform that task in a particular
environment.

109
00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.260
A system may be able to send a message,

110
00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.500
access a database, modify a file, publish
a package,

111
00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.240
or call an external service.

112
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.340
None of those capabilities answers the
question of whether

113
00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.240
it may do so.

114
00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.560
Authority comes from the environment, the
account, the assigned

115
00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.160
role, the approval path, and the limits
placed around

116
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.100
the action. This is why access assumptions
belong in

117
00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.000
the main story rather than in a footnote.

118
00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.740
If a test deliberately provides an open or
unauthenticated

119
00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.500
route, that condition changes what the
result means.

120
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.000
If a system is told that the internet is

121
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.480
unavailable while the network remains
live, the contradiction is

122
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:18.380
part of the incident.

123
00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:22.360
A prepared test can still reveal a real
weakness.

124
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.200
But the weakness may be in the interaction
between

125
00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.560
model, tools, instructions, permissions,
and containment rather than in

126
00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.720
a model independently conquering the
internet.

127
00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.280
That is a more useful finding because it
tells

128
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.940
operators where to look: Are network
boundaries real or

129
00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.320
merely described in a prompt?

130
00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.780
Are credentials scoped to the task?

131
00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.720
Are external actions logged and
interruptible?

132
00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.860
Does the system know when it is operating
on

133
00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.760
a real target? Is there a human who must

134
00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.280
approve a high-consequence action?

135
00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:58.580
What Anthropic’s governance structure
does—and does not—prove Anthropic

136
00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.120
